      body: 'Marisol Reyes was seventeen and had an interview for a summer job at the public library in San Antonio, Texas. Her grandfather said that nobody walks into a real panel without first sitting in front of a pretend one, so on the night before he pulled three chairs up to the kitchen table and called in two cousins. The cousins took it very seriously. One of them wore a tie over his pajamas. The first question was easy: why do you want to work at a library? Marisol talked for four minutes about a book she loved in third grade, then about her favorite author, then about the smell of new paper, and never once said why she wanted the job. Her grandfather held up his hand and read from his Bible: "He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him." (Proverbs 18:13) The cousins laughed so hard one of them fell off his chair. Marisol laughed too, then tried again. Because I like helping people find things, she said, and I am good at keeping things in order, like when I reorganized the youth room shelves at church. Then she stopped. Her grandfather nodded. That, he said, is an answer. The next day the real panel asked the same question, and she gave the same short answer, and smiled the whole time, thinking about the tie and the pajamas. She got the job, and the cousins still ask her the library question at every birthday.',
